/* The application's design tokens.

   ⚠ **The name is the convention and the whole of the configuration.** The build step globs for a
   file called `vixen.ui.vcss` anywhere under the project, finds this, and every class name in every
   `.vxml` and every `.cs` in the project resolves against it. One per project — two would be two
   palettes, which is the thing a token model exists to prevent, so the build refuses rather than
   picking.

   ⚠ **The glob is described rather than written out, and that is not fastidiousness about prose.**
   CSS comments do not nest and they have no escape: the first `*` `/` in a comment closes it, so the
   pattern spelled literally would have ended this comment on its own second line and fed the
   paragraph you are reading to the parser as CSS. It did, for as long as it was written that way —
   `the`, `to`, `is` and a dozen other words were compiled as type selectors, and `bg-brand`,
   `text-brand` and `border-brand` below were rules rather than examples.

   ⚠ **A project with no theme file still has tokens.** Everything below layers over the palette the
   engine ships — Tailwind v4's own twenty-six ramps in oklch — so `bg-slate-800` works here without
   being declared. `--color-*: initial;` is how an application that wants none of them says so, and
   `--color-blue-500: initial;` removes exactly one.

   A token is a custom property, so declaring `--color-brand` does two things at once: it makes
   `var(--color-brand)` available to hand-written CSS, and it tells the generator that `bg-brand`,
   `text-brand` and `border-brand` exist. */

@theme {
    /* 4px, which makes `p-2` eight and `p-4` sixteen and everybody able to see that one is twice the
       other. It is also the engine's default, so this line is a statement rather than a change — and
       it is the one to edit when the whole interface should breathe more. */
    --spacing: 4px;

    /* The one colour this application calls its own. */
    --color-brand: oklch(74% 0.13 195);
}
